The update path is a read-then-write race: `update({ where: { id: existing.id } })` is unconditional, so the `sourceVersion` monotonicity check performed against `existing` (read before the transaction) is not re-validated inside the write. Two concurrent calls with versions 5 and 6 can both pass the pre-check, and the slower/lower (5) transaction can commit last, regressing `sourceVersion`, overwriting `contentHash`/`idempotencyKey`, and re-activating a superseded projection.

Additionally, this transaction sets `idempotencyKey: input.idempotencyKey` on an existing row, which raises P2002 when that key already belongs to another row. Unlike the create branch, this update branch is not wrapped in the `try/catch` P2002 reconciliation, so a key collision escapes as a raw Prisma error instead of `TAXONOMY_CONFLICT`.

Suggest making the write conditional on version and surfacing both cases as typed errors, e.g.:
```ts
const result = await tx.activityExternalClassification.updateMany({
  where: { id: existing.id, sourceVersion: { lt: sourceVersion } },
  data: { ... },
});
if (result.count === 0) throw new StaleError();
```
and wrap the call in the same P2002 reconciliation used for create.

